<title>BELGIUM: EU and Japan settle liquor tax differences.</title>
<headline>EU and Japan settle liquor tax differences.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-02-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>The European Union and Japan have settled a long-running dispute over Japanese tax on European liquor exports.</p>
<p>Taxes on European exports of brown and white spirits, currently up to 60 percent higher than on Japanese products, would be reduced to within three percent, the European Commission said on Tuesday.  </p>
<p>The changes will be introduced in two stages with the first on October 1, 1997, and the second a year later, the Commission said.</p>
<p>Ther EU and Japan have been at odds for more than three years over the tax applied to European liquor exports.</p>
<p>Brown spirits, including cognac and whisky, are currently taxed at up to 600 percent higher than domestic products. This would be brought to within three percent.</p>
<p>White spirits, including vodka and gin, are currently taxed 250 percent higher than domestic products. The differences here would be scrapped, the Commission said.</p>
</text>
